==========================================
DAY 23 NOTES — Disk I/O & Performance Basics
Date: 23-09-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- I/O = reading from / writing to storage; "wa" in top = time CPU spends WAITING on disk
- High "wa" percentage = disk is the bottleneck, not the CPU — a completely different problem
- iostat -x shows detailed per-disk stats; %util near 100 means the disk is maxed out
- iotop shows WHICH process is generating the most disk read/write activity right now
- dd can do a rough write-speed test (dd if=/dev/zero of=testfile bs=1M count=100)
- ALWAYS delete test files created by dd, and never point dd at a real device path by mistake
- Low CPU usage doesn't always mean a fast system — disk I/O can be the hidden bottleneck

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] top (watching wa%)
[ ] iostat -x 1 5
[ ] iotop
[ ] dd speed test

fill this in after running commands yourself :
$ top (note the wa% value)
> 

$ iostat -x 1 5
> 

$ sudo iotop (top disk-using process)
> 


